0 < 1 ---
1 < 1 name: ultrapowers
2 < 1 description: Use when the user runs "/ultrapowers <plan-path>", asks to "execute this plan", "go ultra", or wants an approved plan built autonomously and in parallel — on the exe.dev fleet, never on this machine. Also use when the user runs "/ultrapowers setup", asks whether they have a fleet yet, or asks to build one.
3 < 1 argument-hint: <plan-path> | setup
4 < 1 allowed-tools: Skill Read Grep Glob Bash AskUserQuestion
5 < 1 hooks:
6 < 1   Stop:
7 < 1     - hooks:
8 < 1         - type: command
9 < 1           command: bash "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/keep_working.sh"
10 < 1           timeout: 10
11 < 1 ---
12 < 1 
13 < 1 # Ultrapowers
14 < 1 
15 < 1 This skill is the CLIENT only. Since 0.3.0 there is no LLM engine session:
16 < 1 on the sandbox, a boot unit (`factory/boot.sh`) spawns the one engine, the
17 < 1 Flock (`factory/flock/engine.mjs`), which seeds a board from the plan, runs a
18 < 1 leaderless pool of builders that claim its tasks, merges their copies through
19 < 1 the weave, proves each task by its `Run:` probes and the plan's `Check:` lines,
20 < 1 and decides the merge by its own exit code — code, not prose. Nothing in this
21 < 1 skill runs a plan locally; every dispatch happens on the sandbox.
22 < 1 
23 < 1 The argument decides the mode. A plan path is the client below; the bare word
24 < 1 `setup` is the guided first run. The client falls into setup by itself when the
25 < 1 doctor is not `ready`, so setup is a path through this file and not a separate
26 < 1 errand.
27 < 1 
28 < 1 ## Setup
29 < 1 
30 < 1 The doctor is the only thing that knows which pieces of the fleet are already
31 < 1 there. Every row it returns is a read: running it twice is the same as running
32 < 1 it once, and nothing in setup creates a VM.
33 < 1 
34 < 1 The setup agent uses AskUserQuestion wherever a question can be posed as choices.
35 < 1 The agent runs every command in this section itself. Only three things need a
36 < 1 human — a browser signup, a browser approval, and one copied code — and each of
37 < 1 those is followed by an AskUserQuestion whose recommended option is the next
38 < 1 step, so the agent waits on an answer instead of on a guess. If a command fails
39 < 1 in the agent's own shell, the agent reports the failure and the exact command
40 < 1 and offers, with AskUserQuestion, to hand that one command over; that fallback
41 < 1 exists only after the agent-run form has already failed.
42 < 1 
43 < 1 The first command, run from the plugin cache:
44 < 1 
45 < 1 ```bash
46 < 1 node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/fleet/doctor.mjs --json
47 < 1 ```
48 < 1 
49 < 1 `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` is the installed plugin's directory; Claude Code
50 < 1 writes the real path into this file when it loads it, so the cache path, which
51 < 1 differs by version and by host, never has to be derived by hand. The reference files,
52 < 1 which Claude Code does not rewrite, write the same directory as `<plugin-root>`.
53 < 1 
54 < 1 The doctor answers with one row per piece, and its row ids are `exe-dev`,
56 > 1 `capacity`, `claude`, `accounts`, `github`, `integrations`, `evidence`, `verb-drift`, `cloudflare`, `reaper`, in that
55 < 2 `capacity`, `claude`, `accounts`, `github`, `integrations`, `evidence`, `verb-drift`, `kata`, in that
56 < 1 order. Each row carries
57 < 1 a `status` of `ok` or `missing`, a human `detail`, and a `fix` naming the `## `
58 < 1 section of `references/first-run.md` that repairs it. Read the rows back to the
59 < 1 user as a short list before touching anything. Configuration lives in
60 < 1 `~/.ultrapowers/fleet.json`; the doctor takes `--config <path>` to read it from
61 < 1 somewhere else, and `--target <owner>/<repo>` to include that repository's own
62 < 1 integration object in the answer.
63 < 1 
64 < 1 Then take the red rows in the doctor's order. Open the row's `fix` section in
65 < 1 `references/first-run.md` for the detail — the walk is not restated here — and
66 < 1 say one line about what is happening before each repair.
67 < 1 
68 < 1 `exe-dev` — the account. The human signs up at exe.dev and adds an ssh key in
69 < 1 the browser; that is the first consent and the agent cannot do it for them.
70 < 1 AskUserQuestion: **Done in the browser?** — `Yes, the account and key are in place (Recommended)` / `Not yet, wait for me`.
71 < 1 Then the agent asks, again with AskUserQuestion, which key file `~/.ssh/config`
72 < 1 should point at — `~/.ssh/id_ed25519` (Recommended) or another path the user
73 < 1 names — and writes the `Host *.exe.xyz exe.dev` stanza itself.
74 < 1 
75 < 1 `capacity` — the pool the account's plan allows. When the detail names keys
76 < 1 nothing reads, the agent rewrites the file with cpu and memory only, keeping
77 < 1 any size it already carried, and re-runs the doctor — no question is asked.
78 < 1 The agent reads the doctor's `detail` for the pool it found and asks with
79 < 1 AskUserQuestion: **Lower the run size, or upgrade the plan?** — `Lower it to {"cpu":"4","memory":"8GB"} (Recommended)` / `Upgrade the plan instead`.
80 < 1 On the first the agent writes the smaller size into `~/.ultrapowers/fleet.json`
81 < 1 itself; on the second the human upgrades the plan in the browser and the agent
82 < 1 re-runs the doctor after them.
83 < 1 
84 < 1 `claude` — the token. The agent runs
85 < 1 `node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/fleet/claude-token.mjs login --code-from-clipboard` in the
86 < 1 background; it opens claude.ai and waits for the code to land on the clipboard.
87 < 1 The human approves there and copies the code — the third consent, and the only
88 < 1 secret that ever moves by hand.
89 < 1 AskUserQuestion: **Done in the browser?** — `Yes, the code is copied (Recommended)` / `Not yet, still approving`.
90 < 1 On the answer the agent reads the command's result and says in one line whether
91 < 1 the token landed.
92 < 1 
93 < 1 `github` — the GitHub integration. The agent runs
94 < 1 `ssh exe.dev integrations setup github` and shows the user what it prints; the
95 < 1 human approves the GitHub app install in the browser, which is the second
96 < 1 consent.
97 < 1 AskUserQuestion: **Done in the browser?** — `Yes, the install is approved (Recommended)` / `Not yet, wait for me`.
98 < 1 Keep that integration personal: `--act-as-user` is unavailable on team
99 < 1 integrations, so a team account's PRs are authored by the installation bot
100 < 1 rather than by the user.
101 < 1 
102 < 1 `integrations` — every object a run needs, attached to `tag:fleet`. The agent runs
103 < 1 `node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/fleet/target.mjs <owner>/<repo>` for the repository being
104 < 1 built, which creates the one object that repository needs attached to
105 < 1 `tag:fleet`; the command is idempotent, so an object already there is left
106 < 1 alone and attached only when it is not on `tag:fleet`. When the doctor reports
107 < 1 another object — `claude-max`, say — as not attached, the agent asks with
108 < 1 AskUserQuestion: **Attach `<name>` to the fleet tag?** — `Yes, attach it to tag:fleet (Recommended)` / `No, leave it and I will look`,
109 < 1 and on yes it runs `ssh exe.dev "integrations attach <name> tag:fleet"`.
110 < 1 (`new --integration` is refused since 2026-09-11, and `integrations` has no
111 < 1 `policy` verb, #1434; the tag attachment is the only grant.)
112 < 1 
113 < 1 `evidence` — the operator's evidence repository, where every run's plan and
114 < 1 record live (never on the target, so a run on someone else's repository works
115 < 1 too). It is set up once, in three steps the row checks in order: the key
116 < 1 `"evidence": "<owner>/<repo>"` in `~/.ultrapowers/fleet.json`, the private
117 < 1 repository itself (`gh repo create <owner>/<repo> --private`), and its
118 < 1 integration (`node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/fleet/target.mjs <owner>/<repo>`). The detail
119 < 1 of each step is the `evidence` section of `references/first-run.md`
120 < 1 (`references/first-run.md#evidence`).
121 < 1 
122 < 1 The agent re-runs the doctor after each fix, and the row that turned `ok` is
123 < 1 read back to the user in one line before the next red row is touched. A row
124 < 1 that comes back `missing` twice is reported with the doctor's `detail` and its
125 < 1 walk section, and the agent keeps going down the list rather than starting over.
126 < 1 
127 < 1 When the doctor's verdict is `ready` the fleet is complete, and the launch in
128 < 1 `## Client` below runs in the same turn — setup is a repair, not a destination.
129 < 1 
130 < 1 ## Client
131 < 1 
132 < 1 Selecting ultrapowers at the planning handoff, or invoking `/ultrapowers` on an
133 < 1 approved plan, **is** the authorization to execute — no further approval pause.
134 < 1 
135 < 1 1. **Derive the target from this checkout.** The target is the repository this
136 < 1    skill is run in: `repo` is
137 < 1    `gh repo view --json nameWithOwner -q .nameWithOwner`, and `baseSha` is the
138 < 1    checkout's current commit. There is nothing per-project to configure — the
139 < 1    pair travels in the launch, and each sandbox clones `repo` and branches from
140 < 1    `baseSha`. That sha has to be one GitHub already has, so compare it against
141 < 1    the upstream tip: when they differ, say the base is not on GitHub yet and ask
142 < 1    the operator to push it before the launch. When the plan is for a
143 < 1    repository other than this checkout, the launcher still needs a clone of
144 < 1    that target with it as `origin`: clone it somewhere disposable and add
145 < 1    `--repo <that clone>` to the launch line; `--target` alone is refused. The
146 < 1    launcher compiles the plan from inside that clone, so the plan path on the
147 < 1    launch line resolves relative to it: copy the plan and its
148 < 1    `.gate-verdicts.json` into the clone (an untracked `docs/superpowers/plans/`
149 < 1    there is fine) and name that relative path.
150 < 1 
151 < 1    Then run `node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/fleet/doctor.mjs --target <repo>` once. A
152 < 1    verdict of `ready` goes straight to step 2. Any other verdict is repaired
153 < 1    here: run the `## Setup` path inline, in this same turn, and launch when the
154 < 1    doctor comes back `ready`.
155 < 1 
156 < 1 2. **Launch.** One line:
157 < 1 
158 < 1    ```bash
159 < 1    node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/fleet/launch.mjs <plan-path> --target <repo> --base <baseSha>
160 < 1    ```
161 < 1 
162 < 1    It prints the run id and the VM name — `run-<N>` and `fleet-r<N>-…` — along
163 < 1    with a status URL and the assignment comment; tell the user the run id and
164 < 1    VM name, and point them at step 3 to read progress, not the printed URL —
165 < 1    there is no page behind it today, git is the record. Nothing else needs staging — the launcher commits the plan to the
166 < 1    evidence repository's `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` branch (the target is
167 < 1    never written to until the run's own PR branch), then creates the VM in one lobby call with
168 < 1    `--tag fleet` (the tag every fleet integration is attached to), the
169 < 1    assignment as its comment, and a setup script that starts the run's unit.
170 < 1    No ssh, no second step.
171 < 1 
172 < 1    Add `--hold` to that line when the PR should stay open for a person — a
173 < 1    measurement run; the sandbox then publishes and does not merge.
174 < 1 
175 < 1 3. **Walk away.** The run outlives this session; there is nothing to tail.
177 > 1    While the run is in flight, read it from its evidence record:
176 < 2    While the run is in flight, read it off the board:
177 < 1 
178 < 1    ```bash
180 > 1    node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/fleet/run-read.mjs --run <N> --target <repo>
179 < 2    node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/fleet/board-read.mjs --run <N> --target <repo>
180 < 1    ```
181 < 1 
183 > 1    It prints the run's state, one line per task with its state, then
184 < 1    `== events`: every claim, give-back, objection, conflict, move of main and
185 < 1    the pull request, in order. The sandbox commits the record every 10 s.
182 < 2    It prints `== now` — one line per issue of the run (the run issue and each
183 < 2    task) with its state — then `== events`, the event feed for the run in
184 < 2    order.
185 < 1 
186 < 1    Its state is also `status.json`, written at every transition: `booting` →
187 < 1    `running` → `publishing` → `done`, or `parked` or `failed`. It lives in the
188 < 1    evidence repository (`"evidence"` in `~/.ultrapowers/fleet.json`), under
189 < 1    `runs/<owner>-<repo>/<N>/`. A finished run's record is `status.json` by tag:
190 < 1    `<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>`, the one spelling that keeps working after the VM
191 < 1    is reaped and after the run's live branch is gone.
192 < 1 
193 < 1    ```bash
194 < 1    gh api 'repos/<evidence repo>/contents/runs/<owner>-<repo>/<N>/status.json?ref=<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>' --jq .content | base64 -d
195 < 1    ```
196 < 1 
197 < 1    While the run is in flight that tag is not written yet and the same bytes
198 < 1    are on its `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` branch, a working surface that
200 > 1    goes at the end of the run — `run-read.mjs` reads whichever of the two
201 < 1    holds the run.
199 < 2    goes at the end of the run — read `status.json` there
200 < 2    (`?ref=live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>`) as a fallback when the hub is dark.
201 < 1 
203 > 1    The record is the poll: read it on whatever cadence the user asks for, or
204 < 1    watch it with `--follow`, in the foreground or the background; it ends
205 < 1    by itself when the run's status says `done`, `parked` or `failed`. A watcher
202 < 2    The board is the poll: read it on whatever cadence the user asks for, or
203 < 2    watch it with `--follow`, in the foreground or the background. A watcher
204 < 1    only reads; nothing on this machine writes to a run (step 5).
205 < 1 
206 < 1 4. **The PR is the gate.** There is no approval command. When the engine is
207 < 1    done and the branch is ahead of base, the sandbox pushes it and opens the
208 < 1    PR itself, through the target's integration attached at launch. The run's
209 < 1    code is the `ultra/integration-run-<N>` branch, which is the PR head; its
210 < 1    evidence is under `runs/<owner>-<repo>/<N>/` in the evidence repository, on
211 < 1    the `live/<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` branch while the run is in flight and at
212 < 1    the tag `<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>` once it ends, never merged and linked from
213 < 1    the PR body. Gate-green → a
214 < 1    ready PR and `done`. Parked → a draft PR carrying the gate receipt and
215 < 1    `parked`. `pr` and
216 < 1    `prAuthor` in `status.json` are the PR's URL and who GitHub says opened it —
217 < 1    read both back to the user, and say so when the author is the installation
218 < 1    bot rather than them (their GitHub account is not yet linked on exe.dev's
219 < 1    Integrations page). A ready PR merges itself on the run's own evidence: the
220 < 1    sandbox asks GitHub for no check runs, it merges once its own gate is green
221 < 1    and main has not moved off the tip it folded onto —
222 < 1    `status.json`'s `merged` cell is the squash commit (null when no PR opened) — and `--hold`
223 < 1    on the launch line keeps it open for the operator; a draft PR is the
224 < 1    operator's to merge or close. That self-merge sentence is a gate-green
225 < 1    run's only: after a park the sandbox has exited and merges nothing, so a
226 < 1    parked run is acknowledged by the operator, by hand — mark the draft ready,
227 < 1    `gh pr update-branch <N>` when it is behind main (GitHub's strict rule
228 < 1    refuses a behind merge; this is a GitHub merge, not the kernel's fold),
229 < 1    then `gh pr merge --squash <N>` — or re-driven as a narrower plan. A run
230 < 1    that builds nothing opens no PR. A stories-v1 run whose every proof already
231 < 1    passes at the base ends `done` (phase `nothing to build: every proof already
232 < 1    passes at the base`, `merged` null, tasks closed as already true at the
233 < 1    base). Anything else ends `failed: engine exit <code>`, because a claims-v1
234 < 1    probe already green at the base proves nothing. Either way the record is
235 < 1    pushed and tagged `<owner>-<repo>/run-<N>`. The laptop never fetches a run
236 < 1    branch.
237 < 1 
238 < 1 5. **Reap.** `node ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/fleet/janitor.mjs` removes the VMs of runs
239 < 1    that finished over an hour ago, and reports the stale ones rather than
240 < 1    removing them. The launcher runs it before every launch; nothing schedules it,
241 < 1    and the agent runs it by hand when this machine has been asleep. No
242 < 1    scheduled job or timer on this machine runs the janitor, and none is to be
243 < 1    added: a run merges its own PR from the sandbox and the launcher runs the
244 < 1    reap (#660), so a local process on a timer would only be a second writer,
245 < 1    and one that dies when the laptop sleeps.
246 < 1 
247 < 1 ## Resources
248 < 1 
249 < 1 - `factory/flock/engine.mjs` — the engine (board, builders, weave) as code;
250 < 1   `factory/flock/catchup.mjs` — a finished run caught up to a moved main.
251 < 1 - `references/first-run.md` — one section per doctor row: what it means and the
252 < 1   command that builds it.
253 < 1 - `scripts/plan_parse.py` (the one parser — the sandbox runs it), `scripts/plan_check.py` (the laptop's check of the records and the base, on that parser).
254 < 1 